+## TL;DR
+- **Best for document intake:** [PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR), [MinerU](https://github.com/opendatalab/MinerU), and [docling](https://github.com/docling-project/docling)
+- **Best for searchable policy and knowledge assistants:** [RAGFlow](https://github.com/infiniflow/ragflow), [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm), [Haystack](https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack), and [Qdrant](https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant)
+- **Best for call notes and voice workflows:** [whisper.cpp](https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp), [faster-whisper](https://github.com/SYSTRAN/faster-whisper), and [WhisperX](https://github.com/m-bain/whisperX)
+- **Best low-friction building blocks for custom insurance ops:** [LangChain](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain), [LlamaIndex](https://github.com/run-llama/llama_index), and [Flowise](https://github.com/FlowiseAI/Flowise)
+- **Reality check:** direct GitHub repos built only for insurance agents were scarce, so this list documents the gap honestly and focuses on the strongest open-source capability stack
+
+## Why This List
+Insurance agencies handle scanned ACORD forms, loss runs, declarations pages, policy packets, endorsements, email attachments, call recordings, and knowledge-heavy lookup work. That makes open-source AI unusually attractive for this market: agents need control over data handling, reproducible workflows, and tools they can adapt to their own carrier, MGA, claims, and servicing processes.
+
+This list is built for buyers searching for the **best AI tools for insurance agents in 2026**, but it only includes repositories that actually exist on GitHub. No fake “insurance AI” directories, no closed SaaS listings, and no affiliate filler. If a category is thin, that gap is stated directly.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Document Intake, OCR, and PDF Parsing
+
+#### [PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR)
+> **Description:** PaddleOCR is one of the strongest open-source document AI stacks for insurance document intake. Its README positions it as a toolkit that converts PDFs and images into structured, AI-friendly data such as JSON and Markdown, with support for 100+ languages and broad document parsing features. For insurance teams, that maps well to applications, loss runs, policy schedules, and ID cards where high-volume extraction matters more than a glossy UI. The repository is Apache-2.0 licensed, heavily adopted, and actively maintained, which makes it a safer foundation for production ingestion pipelines than smaller OCR experiments.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR)
+- **Stars:** 66.5k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-01-29
+- **Category:** OCR, document parsing, PDF extraction
+- **Best for:** High-volume OCR and structured extraction from insurance documents
+
+---
+
+#### [tesseract](https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract)
+> **Description:** Tesseract remains the baseline OCR engine for teams that want maximum control and broad language coverage without depending on a hosted vendor. The README highlights support for 100+ languages, multiple output formats including searchable PDF, TSV, ALTO, and hOCR, and both command-line and library use. In an insurance workflow, that makes it valuable as the dependable low-level OCR layer beneath intake systems, searchable archives, and form processing pipelines. It is Apache-2.0 licensed, widely deployed, and mature enough for conservative back-office environments where transparency and predictable behavior matter.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract](https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract)
+- **Stars:** 71.9k ⭐
+- **Language:** C++
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-01-01
+- **Category:** OCR engine, searchable PDF, multilingual OCR
+- **Best for:** Foundational OCR in self-hosted insurance document pipelines
+
+---
+
+#### [MinerU](https://github.com/opendatalab/MinerU)
+> **Description:** MinerU is aimed at turning complex PDFs into LLM-ready Markdown and JSON, which is exactly the sort of preprocessing insurance agencies need before they can build policy search, underwriting copilots, or claims knowledge assistants. Its positioning around complex layouts is important: insurance documents are rarely clean single-column text. Schedules, tables, scanned endorsements, and mixed-layout carrier packets are common. MinerU is open source, Python-based, and actively updated, making it a strong choice when basic OCR is not enough and you need richer document structure preserved for downstream AI retrieval.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/opendatalab/MinerU](https://github.com/opendatalab/MinerU)
+- **Stars:** 50.8k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** AGPL-3.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-12-16
+- **Category:** PDF parsing, OCR, Markdown conversion
+- **Best for:** Converting complex insurance PDFs into RAG-ready content
+
+---
+
+#### [deepdoctection](https://github.com/deepdoctection/deepdoctection)
+> **Description:** deepdoctection is a document understanding library that orchestrates layout analysis, table recognition, and OCR for scanned and PDF documents. Its README explicitly frames it as a package for document understanding and extraction for RAG, which is highly relevant to insurance assistants that need to cite source pages from carrier documents. It supports multiple OCR backends and gives engineering teams more control than end-user OCR products. With Apache-2.0 licensing and active maintenance, it fits insurance environments where teams want to compose a custom ingestion stack instead of relying on a black-box parser.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/deepdoctection/deepdoctection](https://github.com/deepdoctection/deepdoctection)
+- **Stars:** 3.1k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-12-05
+- **Category:** document AI, layout analysis, table extraction
+- **Best for:** Advanced layout-aware parsing of policies, forms, and statements
+
+---
+
+#### [docTR](https://github.com/mindee/doctr)
+> **Description:** docTR describes itself as a seamless and high-performing library for OCR-related tasks powered by deep learning. That matters for insurance teams that need more modern detection-and-recognition pipelines than older OCR engines alone can provide. The project focuses on text detection and text recognition while remaining developer-friendly enough to embed in custom services. Because it is Apache-2.0 licensed and maintained as a reusable library rather than a closed platform, it is a practical building block for agencies or brokers building in-house document extraction around forms, policy documents, and customer-uploaded evidence.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/mindee/doctr](https://github.com/mindee/doctr)
+- **Stars:** 5.7k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-12-14
+- **Category:** OCR, text detection, text recognition
+- **Best for:** Deep-learning OCR components in custom insurance workflows
+
+---
+
+#### [docling](https://github.com/docling-project/docling)
+> **Description:** docling is one of the most useful open-source repos for insurance teams building document-heavy AI workflows. Its README emphasizes parsing multiple formats, advanced PDF understanding, local execution for sensitive data, and export to Markdown, HTML, and JSON. That combination is a strong fit for agencies and carriers dealing with confidentiality, mixed file types, and retrieval pipelines that need structured output instead of raw OCR text. The project is MIT licensed, very actively maintained, and broad enough to support intake pipelines that span PDFs, Office files, audio transcripts, and image attachments.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/docling-project/docling](https://github.com/docling-project/docling)
+- **Stars:** 52.4k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-02-06
+- **Category:** document parsing, PDF understanding, Markdown export
+- **Best for:** Sensitive self-hosted document conversion for insurance knowledge systems
+
+---
+
+#### [dedoc](https://github.com/ispras/dedoc)
+> **Description:** dedoc focuses on automated document parsing into a uniform format and explicitly calls out extraction of content, logical structure, tables, and metadata from PDFs, scans, DOCX, HTML, and other textual documents. That makes it directly useful for insurance operations where consistency matters across many document types coming from carriers, insureds, TPAs, and internal teams. It is not as flashy as some newer AI repositories, but the structured parsing orientation is valuable when an agency needs to normalize incoming paperwork before routing it into search, automation, or compliance workflows.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/ispras/dedoc](https://github.com/ispras/dedoc)
+- **Stars:** 629 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-09-22
