+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for insurance agents in 2026?
+For most teams, the strongest stack is [PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR) or [docling](https://github.com/docling-project/docling) for document intake, [RAGFlow](https://github.com/infiniflow/ragflow) or [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm) for searchable knowledge assistants, and [whisper.cpp](https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp) or [WhisperX](https://github.com/m-bain/whisperX) for call transcription.
+
+### Are there truly open source AI tools built specifically for insurance agents?
+Very few. Direct GitHub results targeted only at insurance agents were sparse, so this list broadens to capability-adjacent tools that insurance teams can self-host and adapt. That gap is real, and pretending otherwise would lower the quality of the repository.
+
+### Which open source AI tools help insurance agents process PDF applications and policy documents?
+Start with [PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR), [MinerU](https://github.com/opendatalab/MinerU), [docling](https://github.com/docling-project/docling), [OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF), and [pdfplumber](https://github.com/jsvine/pdfplumber). Together they cover OCR, parsing, searchable PDFs, and structured extraction.
+
+### Which repositories are best for building an insurance policy chatbot or coverage search assistant?
+[RAGFlow](https://github.com/infiniflow/ragflow), [Haystack](https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack), [LlamaIndex](https://github.com/run-llama/llama_index), [LangChain](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain), [Milvus](https://github.com/milvus-io/milvus), and [Qdrant](https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant) are the strongest open-source building blocks for that use case.
+
+### Can insurance agencies self-host these AI tools for compliance and privacy?
+Yes. That is a major reason to prefer these repositories over closed SaaS tools. Most entries here support self-hosting directly or can be embedded in internal services. Teams still need to validate security, retention, and licensing requirements for their own environment.
+
+### What is the best open source speech-to-text stack for insurance call notes?
+[whisper.cpp](https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp) is the best choice for local offline deployment, [faster-whisper](https://github.com/SYSTRAN/faster-whisper) is excellent for Python batch pipelines, and [WhisperX](https://github.com/m-bain/whisperX) is best when speaker diarization and timestamps matter.
+
+### Are all tools in this list public GitHub repositories?
+Yes. Every entry in this repository links to a public `github.com` URL. Closed SaaS products were excluded on purpose.
+
